  "title": "Real-Life Graphs",
  "practice_data": {
    "method_card": {
      "steps": [
        "Identify the type of graph (distance-time, speed-time, or conversion)",
        "For distance-time: gradient = speed; flat = stationary; downward = return",
        "For speed-time: gradient = acceleration; area under graph = distance",
        "For conversion graphs: read across to the line, then up/down to the other axis",
        "Always include units in your answer"
      ],
      "title": "How to Interpret Real-Life Graphs",
      "content": "<p>Real-life graphs show how one quantity changes with another. The two most common types in GCSE maths are <strong>distance-time</strong> and <strong>speed-time</strong> graphs.</p><p><strong>Distance-time graphs:</strong></p><p>• The <strong>gradient</strong> = speed. A steeper line means faster movement.</p><p>• A <strong>horizontal line</strong> means the object is stationary (speed = 0).</p><p>• A line sloping <strong>downward</strong> means returning to the start.</p><p>$$\\text{Speed} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}} = \\text{gradient of the line}$$</p><p><strong>Speed-time graphs:</strong></p><p>• The <strong>gradient</strong> = acceleration (how quickly speed is changing).</p><p>• A <strong>horizontal line</strong> means constant speed.</p><p>• The <strong>area under the graph</strong> = total distance travelled.</p><p>$$\\text{Distance} = \\text{Area under speed-time graph}$$</p><p><strong>Conversion graphs:</strong> Read across from the known axis, hit the line, then read down (or up) to the other axis. Common examples: currency, temperature, miles/kilometres.</p><p>Common mistake: confusing what the gradient represents — on a distance-time graph it gives speed, but on a speed-time graph it gives acceleration.</p>",
      "example": "<p><strong>A cyclist travels 30 km in 2 hours, then stops for 1 hour, then returns home in 1.5 hours. Find the speed during the first part of the journey.</strong></p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> This is a distance-time graph. Speed = gradient.</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Speed = \\(\\frac{30}{2} = 15\\) km/h</p><p><strong>Check:</strong> During the stop, the gradient is 0 (horizontal line), so speed = 0 ✔</p><p><strong>Return speed:</strong> \\(\\frac{30}{1.5} = 20\\) km/h (faster on the return)</p>"
    },
